A61 — while the guest presents on its override surface, its window's X commits wait

Every game runs inside wine's virtual desktop, so the desktop window is the only
top-level gamescope manages, and the WSI shim binds its override surface
(gamescope_swapchain.override_window_content) to that window. Wine, for a Vulkan
surface it considers offscreen -- Wine 11: a swapchain on a child HWND, always;
Wine 9 without XComposite: once a child window appears -- copies the X client
window into the top-level after every present. On the override route that X
window never receives the guest's pixels, so the copy is black, damages the whole
window, and Xwayland commits it on the main surface right after the shim's
override commit. get_window_last_done_commit() is last-done-wins across both
surfaces, so the panel shows whichever finished last: OMORI (Wine 11, D3D11 on a
child window) was ~95 % black desktop frames, Portal 2 on Wine 9 ~83 %.

update_wayland_res() already drops main-surface commits while an override is
bound, but only those without damage (or every one, with
surface_update_force_only_current_surface or after a non-sRGB colour space).
Forcing that convar on OMORI gave 476 of 476 game frames full-screen at 60 fps,
but as a default it would hide X content for good: a wine message box the game
blocks on, which stops the game presenting, would never show.

So the rule becomes time-based. A window records when its override surface last
committed (frame generation commits arrive on the same surface and count). While
that is within surface_update_override_hold_ms (default 500, 0 = off, env
gamescope_surface_update_override_hold_ms), a damaged main-surface commit that
the existing rule would have accepted is held instead: its presentation feedback
is discarded and the commit is marked done for the client right away (frame
callbacks keep flowing, exactly as the existing drop path), but the wlr_buffer
lock is kept and the entry parked per window. A newer held commit releases the
older one's lock, a main-surface commit that goes through (no override bound any
more) releases it as stale, and destroying the window releases it.

Once the override has been quiet for the hold time, check_new_xwayland_res()
pushes the parked commit through update_wayland_res() before that round's new
commits, so X content reappears without waiting for new X damage (Xwayland does
not re-commit an unchanged window) and anything newer still lands after it. A
parked commit whose surface is no longer the window's main surface, or one the
existing current-surface rule would never show (non-sRGB, the force convar), is
released instead of shown. When the override commits again its frames are
newer and take over.

500 ms: a game that is presenting delivers a frame every 8-50 ms, and a shader
compile or streaming hitch rarely blocks for more than a few hundred; anything
longer is the game not presenting (blocked on a dialog, paused, gone), and a
dialog showing half a second late is fine. A session whose override never
commits (a GL guest that bound a dormant Vulkan swapchain) never holds anything.

One info line each, once per window: "override-hold: ... holds back
main-surface commits" the first time a commit is held, and "override-hold: ...
override quiet for N ms, showing the held main-surface commit" the first time a
held one is shown.

